      "secondary": "Secondary",
      "select_a_constellation_to_see_its_stor": "Select a constellation to see its recognition sketch, story across traditions, observing guide, and science note.",
      "serpens": "Serpens",
      "set_mass_1_m_and_vary_temp_luminosity_": "Set mass = 1 M☉ and vary temp + luminosity. Where does the Sun sit?",
      "seti_home_shut_down_in_2020_after_20_y": "SETI@home shut down in 2020 after 20 years, but Breakthrough Listen makes its data publicly available + the Open SETI project (setisearcher.com) lets volunteers help with signal classification. The SETI Institute (seti.org) has student programs + summer research opportunities. For students at King Middle, this is one of the most accessible advanced research domains — anyone with a laptop can engage with real published data + contribute to the search.",
      "seti_the_actual_searches": "SETI — the actual searches",
      "sextans": "Sextans",
      "shaula": "Shaula",
      "should_we_broadcast_back_meti": "Should we broadcast back? (METI)",
      "shower_activity_zhr": "Shower activity (ZHR)",
      "sirius_a": "Sirius A",
      "sirius_b": "Sirius B",
      "sirius_looks_brightest_in_our_sky_beca": "Sirius LOOKS brightest in our sky because it is close (8.6 light-years), but its absolute magnitude is +1.4 — Vega (also close, 25 ly) is intrinsically brighter at +0.6. Betelgeuse looks medium-bright (+0.5) but is 550 light-years away — its absolute magnitude is -5.8, intrinsically about 100,000× more luminous than the Sun. Without correcting for distance, you cannot compare what stars actually are.",
      "size_scale": "Size + scale",
      "sky_apps": "Sky apps",
      "sky_map": "Sky Map",
      "sky_quality_bortle": "Sky quality (Bortle)",
      "sky_quality_bortle_2": "Sky quality (Bortle)",
      "sky_traditions": "Sky Traditions",
      "skymap_disclaimer": "Positions are computed (schematic, accurate to about a degree). They are geocentric and ignore atmospheric refraction near the horizon, so rise/set timing is approximate. For pinpoint observing use Stellarium or your local planetarium.",
      "skymap_intro": "The real sky overhead, computed for your place and time — Sun, Moon (with its true phase), the five naked-eye planets, and bright reference stars. Hold it over your head facing North: this is an overhead view, so East is on your left.",
      "slide_through_the_lunar_cycle": "Slide through the lunar cycle:",
      "small_galaxies_form_from_the_early_sta": "Small galaxies form from the early stars + dark-matter halos. Mergers + accretion grow them. JWST is now imaging galaxies older than 13.4 billion years (formed within the first ~400 million years of the universe).",
      "smaller_number_brighter_the_historical": " (smaller number = brighter, the historical convention) and ",
