      "first_climb_name": "First Climb",
      "navigation_v2_content": "Modern navigation uses GPS, but the fundamentals haven't changed: heading (compass direction), airspeed, altitude, and time. Great circle routes (the shortest path between two points on a sphere) appear curved on flat maps but are actually straight on a globe. That's why flights from the US West Coast to Europe often pass over Greenland!",
      "one_mile_up_name": "One Mile Up",
      "start_step_first_climb": "Take off and climb to 1,000 ft",
      "start_step_first_climb_go": "Start First Climb",
      "weather_lesson_content": "Weather changes what the air does to your wings. Wind moves the whole air mass, so your airspeed (through the air) and your groundspeed (over the ground) are different numbers: groundspeed is your airspeed plus or minus the wind that points along your path. Into a headwind you cover the ground more slowly, but your wings still feel the same airflow, so you lift off sooner and land in a shorter run. A crosswind pushes you sideways and needs a heading correction to hold your track. Air density matters just as much. Warm, high or humid air is thinner, so the wings make less lift, the propeller bites less and the engine makes less power: takeoffs run longer and climbs are weaker. Pilots call this density altitude, and it is why a hot day at Denver feels like a much higher airport. Cloud, rain and fog cut visibility, storms bring turbulence, and flying through cold cloud can build ice on the wings, which spoils their shape and adds weight. Most light aircraft are not certified for known icing, so the safe plan is to avoid it.",
