      "bernard_c_middleton_a_history_of_engli_2": "Bernard C. Middleton, A History of English Craft Bookbinding Technique (1996)",
      "beyond_guidebooks_bryson_iyer_theroux_": "Beyond guidebooks — Bryson, Iyer, Theroux, Lonely Planet travel-literature. The literary-travel tradition.",
      "beyond_the_falmouth_gazette_the_printe": "Beyond the Falmouth Gazette. The printers of Portland, Bangor, Augusta, Belfast — newspaper presses, book printers, broadside makers across 250 years of Maine print history. Local connection.",
      "bible_translation": "Bible translation",
      "bibliographers_track_30_000_known_incu": "Bibliographers track ~30,000 known incunabula editions; surviving examples are reasonably well catalogued in research libraries.",
      "bigger_formats_quarto_8_pages_per_shee": "Bigger formats (quarto, 8 pages per sheet; octavo, 16 pages) follow the same kind of logic but get progressively harder. In a real 1450 shop, the imposer was a senior craftsman; getting it wrong meant printing the run upside-down or out of order. The schematics for octavo imposition look like geometry puzzles — and they are.",
      "binding": "Binding",
      "binding_type": "Binding type:",
      "bishop_diego_de_landa_yucat_n_1562_ord": "Bishop Diego de Landa (Yucatán, 1562) ordered the burning of Maya codices at Maní — perhaps 5,000 or more books destroyed in a single event. The Aztec library at Texcoco was burned earlier (1530s) by Bishop Juan de Zumárraga. Inca quipu collections were repeatedly burned through the 1500s-1600s.",
      "black_mask_astounding_weird_tales_the_": "Black Mask, Astounding, Weird Tales. The 1920s-50s magazines that birthed sci-fi + hardboiled + many other genres.",
