      "moose_warnings_black_ice_winter_kit_st": "Moose warnings, black ice, winter kit, studded tires.",
      "most_crashes_are_i_didn_t_see_them": "Most crashes are \"I didn't see them\"",
      "most_drivers_set_side_mirrors_to_see_t": "Most drivers set side mirrors to see the back of their own car — that doubles the rearview mirror. Instead: LEAN LEFT until your head almost touches the window, then adjust the left mirror until you just see your car's side. Repeat on the right by leaning right. When sitting normally, you'll see the lanes beside you — REDUCING BLIND SPOTS. Always look over your shoulder too.",
      "motorcycles_and_bicycles_can_hide_in_b": "• Motorcycles and bicycles can hide in blind spots that even cars cannot.",
      "motorcycles_fit_entirely_in_your_a_pil": "Motorcycles fit ENTIRELY in your A-pillar blind spot (the pillar between windshield and door). Bikes + cyclists do the same. Always lean to see past pillars at intersections.",
      "motorcyclists_swerve_for_reasons": "Motorcyclists swerve for reasons",
      "move_over": "Move over:",
      "move_over_law": "Move Over law",
      "move_to_the_right_lane_stay_at_65": "Move to the right lane, stay at 65",
      "mpg_by_speed_chart_label": "Miles per gallon by speed chart",
      "mpg_content": "Miles per gallon (MPG) is a ratio — distance divided by fuel used. Higher = better. But MPG hides something important: the relationship is NON-LINEAR. Going from 15 to 20 MPG saves more fuel than going from 35 to 40 MPG, even though both are a 5 MPG improvement. That is why gallons-per-100-miles (GPM) is sometimes a better metric. Europe uses L/100km for exactly this reason. The things that move your MPG up or down are: speed (sweet spot is a broad 30-55 mph plateau, peaking near 40), aggression (jackrabbit starts = -15 to -40%), tire pressure (low = up to -3%), AC use (-5 to -10% at low speed, negligible at highway), roof racks (-10 to -25%), cargo weight (-1% per 100 lbs), and engine condition.",
      "mpg_vs_speed_graph_beat_the_epa_rating": "MPG vs speed graph — beat the EPA rating.",
      "mpg_vs_speed_steady_cruise": "MPG vs Speed (steady cruise)",
      "multiple_vehicles": "Multiple vehicles:",
      "multiple_vehicles_in_response": "Multiple vehicles in response",
      "must_be_on_before_engine_starts": "Must be on before engine starts.",
      "my_ride_2": "My Ride",
      "my_ride_3": "My Ride",
      "nail_8_10_on_the_right_of_way_scenario": "Nail 8/10 on the Right-of-Way scenarios.",
      "name_the_sign": "🎯 Name the sign",
      "narrow_road_on_a_hill": "Narrow Road on a Hill",
      "navy_blue": "Navy Blue",
      "need_2_drives": "Need 2+ drives",
      "negotiation": "Negotiation",
      "neurodivergent_driver_guide_2": "Neurodivergent Driver Guide",
      "never": "✗ Never: ",
      "never_pass_a_plow_on_the_right_the_bla": "Never pass a plow on the right — the blade throws snow and debris that direction. Stay 4+ truck lengths back. Plows often travel 30–40 mph even on highways; that slow-moving orange light is your safest speed too.",
      "never_say_these_words": "Never Say These Words",
      "never_swerve_1000_lb_rollover": " — never swerve (1000 lb + rollover)",
      "new": "↺ New",
      "next_24_hours": "Next 24 Hours",
      "nhtsa_data": "⚠️ NHTSA data",
      "night_content": "At night your low beams let you see clearly only about 250 feet ahead (Maine driver's manual). High beams reach about twice as far, ~500 feet. On dry pavement your braking distance at 50 mph is about 116 ft, and at night you should budget a 2-second reaction (slower than the 1.5 s daytime figure, because an unlit hazard has to resolve out of darkness before you even recognize it) — that is another ~147 ft, so roughly 263 ft total. That is already MORE than your low beams show, on a dry road with good tires. The manual says it plainly: faster than about 50 mph on a dark road, you are really 'driving blind'. This is called 'overdriving your headlights' and it is how single-vehicle-at-night crashes happen. Rule: drive slow enough that you could stop within the distance you can see. Use high beams only when clear; Maine §2067 requires dimming within 500 ft of oncoming traffic and within 300 ft when following another vehicle.",
      "night_desc": "Suburban roads after dark. Headlights reach ~250 ft with lows, 500 ft with highs. Do not overdrive them.",
      "night_driving": "Night Driving",
