      "met4_sens_aria_attack": "Service attack: dark irregular corrosion marks follow the chromium-depleted grain boundary and meet the exposed surface. Gold diamonds still mark carbides. The cyan surface strip is an oxide film, not an internal grain-boundary layer.",
      "met4_sens_aria_carbides": "Sensitization mechanism: gold carbide diamonds lie at internal grain boundaries. Pale bands beside these boundaries indicate chromium-depleted material. The cyan oxide film is drawn only along the exposed top surface.",
      "met4_sens_aria_initial": "Initial condition: cyan chromium oxide film only at the exposed top surface. White lines inside the metal mark grain boundaries. Blue circles indicate chromium availability, not individual atoms.",
      "met4_sens_attack": "Attack in service",
      "met4_sens_attack_caption": "The chromium-depleted regions are no longer corrosion-resistant. Intergranular corrosion attacks these regions months or years later in service. Dark irregular marks show the attack along grain boundaries, not a crack forming during welding.",
      "met4_sens_available": "Chromium available",
      "met4_sens_available_caption": "Stainless steel resists corrosion because chromium forms a self-healing oxide layer (Cr2O3) on the surface. The cyan strip marks the exposed surface; the lines inside the metal are grain boundaries, not oxide films.",
      "met4_sens_carbides": "Carbides at boundaries",
      "met4_sens_carbides_caption": "Heat between about 800–1500°F causes chromium to combine with carbon, forming chromium carbides (Cr23C6) at grain boundaries. This locally depletes chromium. The gold diamonds mark carbides; pale bands mark depleted material beside the boundaries.",
      "met4_sens_compare": "Compare with initial condition",
      "met4_sens_compare_help": "The initial condition is already selected. Choose a later stage to compare both conditions side by side.",
