      "pipe2_grid_caption": "A passed {value1} test qualifies these production welds:",
      "pipe2_grid_weld": "Production weld",
      "pipe2_groove_img": "Cross-section of a V-groove in a {value1} in. pipe wall: {value2} of {value3} passes placed.",
      "pipe2_groove_legend": "Numbers on beads = the order laid. 1 bevel, 37.5° per side · 2 land (root face), 1/16 in. · 3 root opening, 1/8 in. · 4 wall thickness. ID inside, OD outside.",
      "pipe2_hour_aria": "{value1} o'clock",
      "pipe2_hour_group": "Electrode position, o'clock",
      "pipe2_hour_group_label": "Electrode position (o'clock)",
      "pipe2_k_body": "Body position: ",
      "pipe2_k_puddle": "Puddle: ",
      "pipe2_k_purpose": "Job: ",
      "pipe2_k_rod": "Electrode angle: ",
      "pipe2_k_tech": "Typical technique: ",
      "pipe2_key_large": "pipe 24 in. OD and up only",
      "pipe2_key_no": "not qualified",
      "pipe2_key_yes": "qualified",
      "pipe2_lg_g": "g = gravity (⊗ = into the page)",
      "pipe2_lg_joint": "the joint",
      "pipe2_lg_letters": "Clock numbers = o'clock looking along the pipe. Letters: F flat, H horizontal, V vertical, OH overhead; the coloured band changes with them.",
      "pipe2_lg_ring": "restriction ring (6GR)",
      "pipe2_lg_rod": "electrode + arc",
      "pipe2_matrix_caveat": "Based on the AWS D1.1 welder qualification table for structural steel. ASME Section IX (pressure piping) and API 1104 (pipelines) use their own, different tables, and limits on thickness, diameter, process and backing also apply. Always check the code edition your job is built to.",
      "pipe2_matrix_group": "Qualification test position",
      "pipe2_matrix_intro": "Pick a test position. The grid lights up the production welds a passed test lets you make. Predict first: which tests cover vertical? Which are the only ones that cover a tubular T-connection?",
      "pipe2_matrix_title": "🧩 What does each test qualify?",
      "pipe2_model_1": "Groove area A = g × t + (t − f)² × tan 37.5°, with root opening g = 1/8 in. and land f = 1/16 in. Doubling the wall roughly quadruples the bevel part of the area.",
      "pipe2_model_2": "The root fills the bottom ~1/8 in., the hot pass the next ~3/32 in., the cap the top 1/16 in. Fill passes = the area left ÷ one fill bead, rounded up.",
      "pipe2_model_3": "One fill bead ≈ {value1} sq in.: the Bead Lab's stick model for a 5/32 in. E7018 at about 150 A and 6 in./min. Cap beads: about one per 1/2 in. of cap width. Representative classroom numbers; the WPS governs a real joint.",
      "pipe2_model_title": "How the pass count is estimated",
      "pipe2_mq_4g_who": "Structural, shipyards, bridge",
      "pipe2_mq_4g_why": "Overhead is often called the hardest plate position. Alone, 4G qualifies flat + overhead grooves (and flat, horizontal, overhead fillets); pair it with 3G to cover every plate position.",
      "pipe2_mq_6g_who": "Pipeline, refinery, power plant, nuclear",
      "pipe2_mq_6g_why": "The most challenging common pipe position. Under AWS D1.1 it qualifies all positions for plate and pipe butt welds (T-, K- and Y-connections need 6GR). The \"ace card.\"",
      "pipe2_mq_6gr_who": "Offshore platforms, towers, tubular trusses",
      "pipe2_mq_6gr_why": "A ring fixed beside the joint blocks your rod angle, imitating a branch connection. It is the D1.1 test for CJP T-, K- and Y-connections in tubular structures.",
      "pipe2_mq_asme_who": "Pressure vessel, pharma, food-grade",
      "pipe2_mq_asme_why": "TIG root pass on stainless + carbon steel pipe = the gold standard of pipe welding.",
      "pipe2_note_3g": "3G covers flat, horizontal and vertical — everything but overhead. Add 4G for overhead.",
      "pipe2_note_3g4g": "3G + 4G together: every plate groove and fillet position. Pipe butt joints still only on large pipe (24 in. OD and up).",
      "pipe2_note_4g": "Alone, 4G covers flat + overhead grooves — not horizontal or vertical. Add 3G (3G + 4G) to cover every plate groove position.",
      "pipe2_note_5g": "5G covers flat, vertical and overhead — the positions round a fixed horizontal joint — but not horizontal. Add 2G (2G + 5G) for all positions.",
      "pipe2_note_6g": "6G: all positions for plate and pipe butt welds. Not CJP T-, K- or Y-connections — those need 6GR.",
      "pipe2_note_6g_slope": "the axis is tilted 45°, so every o'clock also has a slope: the puddle tends to roll toward the lower pipe. Angle the rod slightly up-slope and favour the upper bevel.",
      "pipe2_note_6gr": "6GR: everything 6G covers, plus CJP T-, K- and Y-connections. The restriction ring imitates a branch connection's cramped access.",
      "pipe2_note_6gr_ring": "the ring beside the joint blocks your rod on one side, as the brace wall of a T-, K- or Y-connection would. Expect steeper angles and short, careful manipulation.",
      "pipe2_note_fillet": "Fillet tests qualify fillet welds only — never a groove weld, in any position.",
      "pipe2_note_pipe": "Pipe groove tests also qualify plate grooves and fillets in the same positions.",
      "pipe2_note_plate": "Plate groove tests also qualify fillets, but reach pipe butt joints only on large pipe (24 in. OD and up).",
      "pipe2_o_cap_fill": "The groove is already filled to the cap line. Another fill pass would leave no room for a clean cap and pile up excess reinforcement.",
      "pipe2_o_cap_other": "Already done. What is left is the cap.",
      "pipe2_o_done": "The joint is complete. Reset to build another, or change the wall thickness.",
      "pipe2_o_fill_cap": "Not full yet. Fill until the groove is about 1/16 in. below the surface; a cap over an underfilled groove ends up concave or below flush, an underfill reject.",
      "pipe2_o_fill_done": "That pass is already in. The groove now needs fill passes, layered from the bottom up, with slag cleaned off between every pass.",
      "pipe2_o_head": "✗ Not the {value1} yet. ",
      "pipe2_o_hot_cap": "The groove is nearly empty: a cap here would sag into it, and the root still needs its hot pass to burn out trapped slag.",
      "pipe2_o_hot_fill": "Hot pass first. It burns out slag trapped by the root's wagon tracks and fuses the root to the bevel walls. A cooler fill pass laid straight on the root seals that slag in.",
      "pipe2_o_one_root": "The root is already in, and a joint gets one root pass. Next comes the hot pass.",
      "pipe2_o_root_any": "Start at the bottom. With no root pass the root opening is still open: metal would fall through or bridge over it, leaving incomplete penetration that RT finds at once.",
      "pipe2_o_root_hot": "Nothing to burn into yet. The hot pass is laid ON the root, so first the root pass must close the root opening and fuse both lands.",
      "pipe2_o_thin": "On a wall this thin, root + hot pass already reach the cap line: no fill pass is needed. Go straight to the cap.",
      "pipe2_p_cap": "Cap pass",
      "pipe2_p_cap_purpose": "Finishes the joint with a slight crown of reinforcement and clean, fused toes: the surface an inspector sees first.",
      "pipe2_p_cap_tech": "Beads (or a controlled weave) that reach about 1/16 in. past each bevel edge, overlapping each other by about half, with no undercut at the toes.",
      "pipe2_p_fill": "Fill pass",
      "pipe2_p_fill_purpose": "Builds the groove up, bead by bead and layer by layer, to about 1/16 in. below the surface.",
      "pipe2_p_fill_tech": "E7018 low-hydrogen (or FCAW) stringers or a slight weave. Clean slag between EVERY pass and keep interpass temperature within the WPS.",
      "pipe2_p_hot": "Hot pass",
      "pipe2_p_hot_purpose": "Burns out slag trapped in the \"wagon tracks\" along the root's edges, fuses the root to the bevel walls and adds strength so the thin root does not crack.",
      "pipe2_p_hot_tech": "Hotter and faster than the root, laid soon after it (on pipelines within minutes). Grind the root's high spots first.",
      "pipe2_p_root": "Root pass",
      "pipe2_p_root_purpose": "Closes the root opening and fuses both lands, with full penetration to the inside of the pipe. Radiography judges this pass most strictly.",
      "pipe2_p_root_tech": "E6010 stick (keyhole, whip and pause) on much carbon-steel pipe, or GTAW (TIG) on critical and stainless pipe. Keep the keyhole small and the inside bead even.",
      "pipe2_pause": "⏸ Pause",
