      "pipe2_stack_count": "Passes placed: {value1} of {value2}",
      "pipe2_stack_done": "✓ Joint complete: {value1} passes (1 root, 1 hot, {value2} fill, {value3} cap). Now pick another wall thickness and predict the count before you build it.",
      "pipe2_stack_intro": "Build this pipe weld yourself, one pass at a time, in the order a welder would. Each pass has its own job. Get the order wrong and you'll hear why.",
      "pipe2_std_d11": "AWS D1.1 welder performance qualification",
      "pipe2_std_math": "CCSS Math: rates, area + rounding up",
      "pipe2_step": "Step ⏭",
      "pipe2_tab_bend": "↩ Bend test",
      "pipe2_tab_matrix": "🧩 What a test qualifies",
      "pipe2_tl_fillet": "{value1} fillet",
      "pipe2_tl_none": "Nothing more: 6G already covers it",
      "pipe2_tl_pipe": "{value1} pipe",
      "pipe2_tl_plate": "{value1} plate",
      "pipe2_tn_m4": "\"5G is one pass all the way round the pipe\" — uphill 5G is welded in two halves, each from 6 o'clock (bottom) up to 12 o'clock (top).",
      "pipe2_tn_m5": "\"6GR is just a harder 6G for nuclear work\" — 6GR adds a restriction ring; it is the AWS D1.1 test for CJP T-, K- and Y-connections in tubular structures.",
      "pipe2_tn_q4": "On a 5G joint each uphill half starts at 6 o'clock and ends at 12. Why not weld all the way round in one direction?",
      "pipe2_tn_q5": "In the pass stacker, compare a 0.432 in. wall with a 0.719 in. wall. Why does the fill count grow faster than the wall thickness?",
      "pipe2_tn_qp_m4": "\"A 4G plate test covers every position\" — alone it qualifies flat + overhead grooves (and F/H/OH fillets). It takes 3G + 4G to cover every plate groove position.",
      "pipe2_tn_qp_m5": "\"Undercut means too much heat input\" — fast travel LOWERS heat input. Undercut comes from too much current or voltage, travel too fast for the current, or a poor electrode angle.",
      "pipe2_tn_qp_q4": "Use the qualification matrix: which single plate test covers the most work? Which pair covers every plate position? Why does a fillet test never qualify a groove weld?",
      "pipe2_tn_qp_q5": "A bend specimen shows a 3/32 in. open discontinuity on its stretched face. Accept or reject under the commonly cited D1.1 limit? What about 3/16 in.?",
      "pipe2_travel_down_read": "Both halves (12 → 3 → 6 and 12 → 9 → 6): {value1}%",
      "pipe2_travel_down_txt": "Downhill \"stovepipe\": cellulosic E6010/E8010 on cross-country pipelines, under API 1104 procedures. Drag angle, fast travel, often two welders on opposite sides at once. Faster and less heat per inch, so the procedure must account for the shallower fusion.",
      "pipe2_travel_legend": "Numbered dots: half 1 and half 2. Dashed track: still to weld. The rod leans with the technique: tipped up the pipe for uphill, a drag angle for downhill.",
      "pipe2_travel_title": "Watch the halves on a fixed (5G) joint",
      "pipe2_travel_up_read": "Half 1 (6 → 9 → 12): {value1}% · Half 2 (6 → 3 → 12): {value2}%",
      "pipe2_travel_up_txt": "Uphill: typically E7018 low-hydrogen (or an E6010 root) on structural and pressure pipe. Rod tipped slightly up toward travel; a small weave or shelf builds each ripple on the one below. Slower, more heat per inch, deeper fusion. Each half starts just past 6 so the second half ties in over a feathered start.",
      "pipe2_u_in2": "sq in.",
      "pipe2_undo": "↶ Undo",
      "pipe2_wall_2s80": "2 in. Sch 80",
      "pipe2_wall_6s160": "6 in. Sch 160",
      "pipe2_wall_6s80": "6 in. Sch 80",
      "pipe2_wall_hint": "Thicker wall, bigger groove, more fill passes. Passes already placed are kept if they still fit the new plan.",
      "pipe2_wall_label": "Pipe wall thickness",
      "pipe2_wall_preset": "{value1} ({value2} in.)",
      "pipe2_wall_val": "{value1} in.",
      "pipe2_where": "Where: ",
      "pipe2_why": "Why: ",
      "pipe2_z_away": "Not welded here in 1G",
      "pipe2_z_away_body": "Stay put at the top; let the rollers bring the joint to you.",
      "pipe2_z_away_puddle": "This is why 1G is the easiest pipe test, and why it qualifies only flat-position groove welds.",
      "pipe2_z_away_rod": "In 1G you never weld here: roll the pipe until this spot comes up to the top.",
      "pipe2_z_flat": "Flat (top of the pipe)",
      "pipe2_z_flat_body": "Standing over the pipe, looking down into the joint.",
      "pipe2_z_flat_puddle": "Gravity pulls the puddle THROUGH the root: the risk here is excess penetration (\"icicles\") and burn-through, made worse by heat built up from below.",
      "pipe2_z_flat_rod": "Rod pointing down into the joint with a small drag angle (about 5-15°). Travel a little faster and watch the keyhole.",
      "pipe2_z_horizontal": "Horizontal (2G, all the way round)",
      "pipe2_z_horizontal_body": "Walk or shuffle round the pipe. The back side is the hard part: reposition or switch hands.",
      "pipe2_z_horizontal_puddle": "The puddle sags onto the lower bevel: undercut on the upper toe, overlap on the lower. Stack stringer beads from the bottom up.",
      "pipe2_z_horizontal_rod": "Rod roughly level, angled slightly up (about 5-15°) to hold metal on the upper bevel, with a small drag along travel.",
      "pipe2_z_lower": "Overhead-to-vertical transition",
      "pipe2_z_lower_body": "Kneeling or crouching low beside the pipe so you can see the leading edge of the puddle.",
      "pipe2_z_lower_puddle": "The puddle starts to sag away from the joint. Keep it small and let the edges freeze.",
      "pipe2_z_lower_rod": "Rod near 90° to the surface, angled slightly up toward travel; keep the arc tight as the pipe curves.",
      "pipe2_z_overhead": "Overhead (bottom of the pipe)",
      "pipe2_z_overhead_body": "Under the pipe looking up, head off to one side, out of the way of falling slag and spatter.",
      "pipe2_z_overhead_puddle": "Gravity pulls the puddle AWAY from the root: watch for a concave root inside the pipe (suck-back) and a sagging, convex face.",
      "pipe2_z_overhead_rod": "Rod almost straight up into the joint, tipped 5-10° toward travel. Short arc; push the puddle up into the root.",
      "pipe2_z_rolled": "Flat, pipe rolled (1G)",
      "pipe2_z_rolled_body": "Standing still: the pipe turns on rollers or a positioner, so your body hardly moves.",
      "pipe2_z_rolled_puddle": "A flat-position puddle: the easiest to control and the fastest to deposit.",
      "pipe2_z_rolled_rod": "Rod near vertical with a slight drag. You weld at the top while the pipe turns under you.",
      "pipe2_z_upper": "Vertical-to-flat transition",
      "pipe2_z_upper_body": "Rising up and leaning over the pipe. Stop and reposition before your wrist runs out of travel.",
      "pipe2_z_upper_puddle": "Heat from the weld below builds up: the puddle gets bigger and more fluid. Speed up slightly to keep it small.",
      "pipe2_z_upper_rod": "Keep the rod near 90° to the pipe surface and roll your wrist as the surface turns toward flat.",
      "pipe2_z_vertical": "Vertical",
      "pipe2_z_vertical_body": "Standing or crouching beside the pipe, eyes level with the joint, elbow free.",
      "pipe2_z_vertical_puddle": "The puddle wants to run downhill. Build a shelf: each ripple freezes and holds up the next.",
      "pipe2_z_vertical_rod": "Rod near 90° to the surface, tipped about 5-15° up toward travel. A short side-to-side motion with a pause at each bevel wall.",
      "pipe2_zone_head": "{value1} at {value2} o'clock: {value3}",
      "pipe3_area_note": "Areas describe the allocated spaces inside this classroom groove drawing. They exclude cap and root reinforcement, bead overlap and remelting. They are not deposited-metal measurements or a quality score; the WPS governs a real weld.",
      "pipe3_cumulative": "Occupied through this pass",
      "pipe3_empty": "Place the root pass below to start inspecting the build. Each correctly placed pass opens another inspection point.",
      "pipe3_groove_selection": "Pass {value1}, {value2}, is outlined. Later laid passes are dimmed.",
      "pipe3_group": "Inspect a laid pipe weld pass",
      "pipe3_intro": "Select a laid pass to outline it in the groove. Earlier passes stay solid; later laid passes dim. Inspection changes the view, while your built stack stays intact.",
      "pipe3_progress": "Groove occupied through the inspected pass: {value1}%",
      "pipe3_remaining": "Groove remaining at this step",
      "pipe3_selected": "Pass {value1} · {value2}",
      "pipe3_this_area": "This pass, inside groove",
      "pipe3_title": "Inspect the passes you laid",
