      "proc2_intro2": "Look inside each process first, then set up a job in the Job Matcher and watch the processes re-rank. The decision guide at the bottom asks you to predict before it tells you.",
      "proc2_j2d_aria": "{value1}, cross-section: {value2}",
      "proc2_j3d_aria": "{value1}, 3/4 view: {value2}",
      "proc2_j_butt_def": "Incomplete penetration at the root, burn-through on thin plate, undercut at the toes",
      "proc2_j_butt_uses": "Pipe, pressure vessels, ship-hull plate, structural beam splices, frame rails",
      "proc2_j_butt_welds": "Groove welds: square, single-V, single-bevel, J or U (pick one below to see it). Fillet welds are not used on butt joints, because there is no corner to put one in.",
      "proc2_j_corner_def": "Burn-through at the corner edge where heat concentrates, poor fit-up gaps, root cracking",
      "proc2_j_corner_uses": "Boxes and enclosures, toolboxes, cabinets, tanks, frame corners",
      "proc2_j_corner_welds": "An open corner (edges meeting corner to corner, as drawn) is welded on the outside, filling the V the edges make, and can take an inside fillet too. A closed corner, with one plate flush over the other's end, takes a fillet on the inside.",
      "proc2_j_edge_def": "Burn-through on very thin sheet, warping from local heat, an incomplete seal",
      "proc2_j_edge_uses": "Thin sheet-metal tanks and containers, HVAC plenums, flanged sheet edges, trim",
      "proc2_j_edge_welds": "An edge weld melted across the aligned edges, sometimes after the edges are flanged. Usually a light seal weld rather than one that carries load.",
      "proc2_j_lap_def": "Lack of fusion into the bottom plate, melting away the top plate's edge, overlap",
      "proc2_j_lap_uses": "Auto-body panels, HVAC ductwork, sheet-metal fabrication, gussets, aluminum boat hulls",
      "proc2_j_lap_welds": "Fillet welds along the edge of the overlapping plate, on one or both ends. Plug or slot welds through the top plate, or spot welds on sheet.",
      "proc2_j_tee_def": "Undercut on the upright plate, overlap on the flat plate, unequal fillet legs, lack of root fusion",
      "proc2_j_tee_uses": "Structural framing, machine bases, brackets, stiffeners, ship frames, table legs",
      "proc2_j_tee_welds": "Fillet welds on one or both sides. Where full penetration is required, the edge of the upright plate is beveled for a groove weld, often with a reinforcing fillet.",
