      "dx_reef_ball_behavior": "Holes and a hollow interior provide shelter while the outer surface can be colonized.",
      "dx_reef_ball_diet": "This concrete structure does not feed. Attached organisms obtain food from the surrounding water.",
      "dx_reef_ball_habitat": "Prepared reef or restoration areas, placed on the seafloor as a hollow shelter structure.",
      "dx_reef_ball_observe": "Look for the rounded dome and repeated openings, then notice which animals are actually using them.",
      "dx_reef_manta_behavior": "May gather to feed and circle slowly over a cleaning station.",
      "dx_reef_manta_diet": "Filters tiny drifting animals from the water as it swims with its mouth open.",
      "dx_reef_manta_habitat": "Reef-associated waters with plankton-rich feeding areas and established cleaning stations.",
      "dx_reef_manta_observe": "Observe its shoulder markings and belly spots during a natural pass, leaving its route open.",
      "dx_reef_star_behavior": "The frame supports planted fragments and gives settling coral larvae a more stable surface.",
      "dx_reef_star_diet": "This is restoration equipment. Corals attached to it feed and grow as living animals.",
      "dx_reef_star_habitat": "Restoration areas with unstable coral rubble, where linked frames can hold the bottom steady.",
      "dx_reef_star_observe": "Find the six-sided coated frame and the small coral fragments tied along its bars.",
      "dx_regal_angelfish_behavior": "Usually encountered alone or in pairs, occasionally in small groups. Young fish shelter in coral cracks and crevices.",
      "dx_regal_angelfish_diet": "Feeds on sponges and tunicates, including sea squirts.",
      "dx_regal_angelfish_habitat": "Coral-rich lagoons and seaward reefs, often around caves and ledges. The species ranges from East Africa and the Red Sea across the tropical Indo-Pacific to the Tuamotu region.",
      "dx_regal_angelfish_observe": "Follow the bars down this adult’s sides, then compare the emperor angelfish’s finer, mostly horizontal stripes. Check the tail, fin patterns and chest colour together; juveniles and regional forms differ.",
      "dx_review_filter_all": "All organisms ({n})",
      "dx_review_filter_count": "Showing {shown} of {total} organisms from this round.",
      "dx_review_filter_label": "Choose which organisms to review",
      "dx_review_filter_missed": "Missed or revealed ({n})",
      "dx_ribbon_eel_behavior": "Usually shelters with its head or part of its slender body protruding from a burrow. This resting view shows an exposed forebody; much of the animal remains concealed.",
      "dx_ribbon_eel_diet": "Small fishes. It is a predator rather than a plankton-feeding garden eel.",
      "dx_ribbon_eel_habitat": "Tropical Indo-West Pacific lagoon and seaward reefs, using burrows in sand or rubble beside coral reefs.",
      "dx_ribbon_eel_observe": "Find the flared nostrils and little jaw projections first, then compare its narrow ribbon-shaped body with the giant moray’s heavier head and mottled body. Black, blue-and-yellow and largely yellow appearances are reported, so combine shape and colour rather than relying on one hue.",
