      "dx_saved_filter": "Saved species",
      "dx_sea_cucumber_behavior": "Its slow feeding mixes the surface sediment, and it often shelters during the day.",
      "dx_sea_cucumber_diet": "Swallows sediment to digest organic films and other material coating its grains.",
      "dx_sea_cucumber_habitat": "Shallow tropical reef sand and rubble, with shelter beneath stones or coral slabs.",
      "dx_sea_cucumber_observe": "Look for the smooth dark back and pink lower side, then trace its position along the sand.",
      "dx_sea_fan_behavior": "The colony grows as a connected net, creating habitat for very small reef animals.",
      "dx_sea_fan_diet": "Tiny polyps capture plankton carried through the fan by the current.",
      "dx_sea_fan_habitat": "Current-swept reef slopes and walls where water passes through its broad branching mesh.",
      "dx_sea_fan_observe": "Observe the open mesh and its broadside orientation, then compare it with a bushy soft coral.",
      "dx_second_look_earlier": "Your first collection",
      "dx_second_look_earlier_help": "Your original answers and observations stay here for comparison during this practice session.",
      "dx_second_look_help": "Only the organisms you missed or revealed return. Take your time, and look for a detail you remember.",
      "dx_second_look_offer": "Your next round can focus just on these {n} missed or revealed organisms.",
      "dx_second_look_recognized": "Recognized after another look",
      "dx_second_look_reflect": "What became easier to recognize? Compare this round with your first collection below.",
      "dx_second_look_title": "A focused second look",
      "dx_selection_position": "Entry {n} of {total} in this selection",
      "dx_show_choices": "Show identification choices",
      "dx_soft_coral_behavior": "Its fleshy branches expand in flowing water and can become less erect when the current eases.",
      "dx_soft_coral_diet": "Captures drifting food with its polyps; Dendronephthya lacks sunlight-feeding algal partners.",
      "dx_soft_coral_habitat": "Reef places with flowing water that carries suspended food to its branching crown.",
      "dx_soft_coral_observe": "Compare the soft tree-shaped crown and clustered tip polyps with a sea fan's flatter net.",
      "dx_sources": "Sources and further reading",
      "dx_spotted_garden_eel_behavior": "Rises partway from its burrow to feed, generally facing into the current with neighboring eels. It retreats rapidly when threatened.",
      "dx_spotted_garden_eel_diet": "Zooplankton carried past its burrow by the current.",
      "dx_spotted_garden_eel_habitat": "Sandy flats and slopes bordering tropical Indo-Pacific coral reefs, often in colonies.",
      "dx_spotted_garden_eel_observe": "Follow the thin, spotted body from its sandy opening to the head. Look for the larger dark patch behind the head among the tiny spots.",
      "dx_spotted_seahorse_behavior": "Often grips vegetation or another support with its prehensile tail. Small dorsal and pectoral fins help it move and steer; it can leave its holdfast to swim.",
      "dx_spotted_seahorse_diet": "Tiny crustaceans and other zooplankton, drawn through the tubular snout by suction.",
      "dx_spotted_seahorse_habitat": "Indo-West Pacific coastal habitats, including Singapore estuaries with seagrass and macroalgae over silty sand. It also occurs around sheltered sandy and reef habitats.",
      "dx_spotted_seahorse_observe": "Compare the upright trunk and curled gripping tail with the banded pipefish’s slender body and distinct tail fin. Then inspect the rounded crown and thick snout. Yellow is only one appearance: Singapore adults also occur in greenish brown with dark dots.",
      "dx_staghorn_coral_behavior": "Growing branches extend the colony and create shelter for small reef fishes.",
      "dx_staghorn_coral_diet": "Algal partners supply energy, and the animal polyps also capture tiny drifting food.",
      "dx_staghorn_coral_habitat": "Reef slopes and lagoons where branching Acropora forms thickets with sheltered spaces between branches.",
      "dx_staghorn_coral_observe": "Look for a larger cup at each branch tip and many smaller cups along the sides.",
      "dx_table_coral_behavior": "Fine upward branchlets form a living canopy that shades the water beneath it.",
      "dx_table_coral_diet": "Algal partners supply energy, and the animal polyps also capture tiny drifting food.",
      "dx_table_coral_habitat": "Upper reef slopes and outer reef flats, where flat branching colonies form broad tables.",
      "dx_table_coral_observe": "Examine the brush-like upper surface and central support before comparing it with a layered foliose coral.",
      "dx_threadfin_butterflyfish_behavior": "The trailing back-fin filament becomes a useful clue on adults; regional markings can vary.",
      "dx_threadfin_butterflyfish_diet": "Uses its small mouth to pick worms, anemones, coral polyps and algae from the reef.",
      "dx_threadfin_butterflyfish_habitat": "Coral reef habitats across the tropical Indo-Pacific, from sheltered reef areas to outer reefs.",
      "dx_threadfin_butterflyfish_observe": "Find the eye band, fine angled lines and rear eyespot together; color alone is less reliable.",
      "dx_title": "Your marine field guide",
      "dx_try_practice": "Try identification practice",
      "dx_unsaved": "Removed from your saved species.",
      "dx_whitetip_reef_shark_behavior": "Can breathe while resting on the bottom; it becomes more active as a hunter around dusk.",
      "dx_whitetip_reef_shark_diet": "Hunts fishes, octopuses, lobsters and crabs, including prey concealed in reef cracks.",
      "dx_whitetip_reef_shark_habitat": "Reef caves, ledges and nearby sandy resting areas in tropical waters.",
      "dx_whitetip_reef_shark_observe": "Check the white first-dorsal and upper-tail tips, broad short snout and slender body together.",
      "dx_yellow_boxfish_behavior": "The rigid, plated body changes color and shape with age, while small fins provide control.",
      "dx_yellow_boxfish_diet": "Takes algae and a variety of small animals living on or near the bottom.",
      "dx_yellow_boxfish_habitat": "Relatively sheltered coastal and offshore reefs with places to hover and forage.",
      "dx_yellow_boxfish_observe": "Identify the box-like outline first; bright yellow with black spots is the juvenile pattern.",
      "dx_yellow_longnose_butterflyfish_behavior": "Often encountered in pairs, with single fish and small groups also reported.",
      "dx_yellow_longnose_butterflyfish_diet": "Small crustaceans, hydroids, worm tentacles and echinoderm tube feet; also fish eggs.",
      "dx_yellow_longnose_butterflyfish_habitat": "Reef flats, crests, slopes and sheltered ledges across the tropical Indo-Pacific and into the eastern Pacific.",
      "dx_yellow_longnose_butterflyfish_observe": "Follow the narrow snout to the two-tone head. Compare its yellow body with the copperband butterflyfish’s bars, then locate the spot below the tail.",
