      "fig_ac_2010": "By 2010: about 3.5",
      "fig_ac_85": "about 85%",
      "fig_ac_aria": "Scale of aragonite saturation from 0 to 5. In tropical waters it was about 4.2 before industry and about 3.5 by 2010. Above 1, corals can still build. Below, building speed: each 1 unit drop makes corals build roughly 15% slower, about 85% of the speed before, and studies vary widely.",
      "fig_ac_lower": "1 unit lower",
      "fig_ac_one": "Above 1, corals can still build",
      "fig_ac_pre": "Before industry: about 4.2",
      "fig_ac_speed": "Building speed for each 1 unit drop",
      "fig_ac_spread": "Studies vary widely.",
      "fig_ac_start": "Start",
      "fig_ac_title": "Aragonite saturation",
      "fig_bl_aria": "Diagram. A healthy coral kept in water that is too warm for too long loses its algae and turns white: it is bleached, alive but starving. If the water cools in time, the algae return. If the heat goes on too long, the coral dies. Below, the 2016 Great Barrier Reef heatwave: reefs with under about 3 Degree Heating Weeks lost almost no coral, and reefs with about 8 lost about two thirds of their coral within eight months.",
      "fig_bl_at8": "About 8 DHW",
      "fig_bl_bleached": "Bleached",
      "fig_bl_cools": "Water cools in time",
      "fig_bl_dhw": "DHW: Degree Heating Weeks",
      "fig_bl_dies": "Coral dies",
      "fig_bl_gbr": "Great Barrier Reef, 2016",
      "fig_bl_gbr_sub": "Coral lost within eight months",
      "fig_bl_healthy": "Healthy",
      "fig_bl_heat_lasts": "Heat goes on too long",
      "fig_bl_none_lost": "almost none lost",
      "fig_bl_recovers": "Algae return",
      "fig_bl_starving": "alive but starving",
      "fig_bl_too_warm": "Too warm for too long",
      "fig_bl_two_thirds": "about two thirds lost",
      "fig_bl_under3": "Under about 3 DHW",
      "fig_fo_after": "After: some coral, fewer kinds",
      "fig_fo_aria": "Three coral shapes in the 1998 bleaching off Okinawa. Branching corals were hit hardest, while many boulder and encrusting corals survived. These are tendencies: boulder corals can die too. Below, a reef before and after: it keeps some coral but has fewer kinds.",
      "fig_fo_before": "Before: many kinds",
      "fig_fo_boulder": "Boulder",
      "fig_fo_hardest": "hit hardest",
      "fig_fo_survived": "many survived",
      "fig_fo_tendency": "These are tendencies: boulder corals can die too.",
      "fig_fo_title": "Okinawa, 1998 bleaching",
      "fig_gr_aria": "Cause and effect diagram. Grazing fish eat seaweed and keep space open where young corals can settle. Nutrients from land feed the seaweed, and seaweed crowds out young corals. Below, a marine reserve in the Bahamas where parrotfish were protected: grazing was twice as high as on a fished reef, and seaweed cover was about a quarter as high.",
      "fig_gr_bahamas": "Bahamas marine reserve",
      "fig_gr_cover": "Seaweed cover",
      "fig_gr_crowds": "crowds out",
      "fig_gr_eat": "eat",
      "fig_gr_feed": "feed",
      "fig_gr_fished": "Fished reef",
      "fig_gr_grazers": "Grazing fish",
      "fig_gr_grazing": "Grazing",
      "fig_gr_keep_open": "keep space open",
      "fig_gr_nutrients": "Nutrients from land",
      "fig_gr_protected": "parrotfish protected",
      "fig_gr_quarter": "about 1/4",
      "fig_gr_reserve": "Reserve",
      "fig_gr_seaweed": "Seaweed",
      "fig_gr_twice": "twice",
      "fig_gr_young": "Young corals need open space",
      "fig_hb_aria": "A claim and its tests. The inventors of Biorock, steel frames with a gentle electric current, report that corals grow faster and survive heat better. Careful tests with fair comparisons in Australia, Kenya and the Philippines mostly found no lasting benefit, and in one case found harm. A fair test compares frames with and without the current, with everything else the same. Good science means checking big claims with fair tests.",
      "fig_hb_check": "Good science checks big claims with fair tests.",
      "fig_hb_claim": "The claim",
      "fig_hb_fair": "A fair test changes one thing",
      "fig_hb_faster": "corals grow faster",
      "fig_hb_harm": "one case: harm",
      "fig_hb_heat": "survive heat better",
      "fig_hb_inventors": "reported by the inventors",
      "fig_hb_none": "mostly no lasting benefit",
      "fig_hb_places": "Australia, Kenya, Philippines",
      "fig_hb_same": "same corals, same place",
      "fig_hb_tests": "Fair tests",
      "fig_hb_with": "with the current",
      "fig_hb_without": "without it",
      "fig_hf_aria": "Parts of the Great Barrier Reef in 2022 and 2024. In 2022 they had their highest coral cover in 36 years, much of it fast-growing branching and table corals. In 2024 heat, cyclones and starfish caused the largest one-year drops on record. Recovery is real, and it lasts longest when heatwaves ease.",
      "fig_hf_causes": "after heat, cyclones and starfish",
      "fig_hf_drop": "largest one-year drops on record",
      "fig_hf_ease": "Recovery is real, and it lasts longest when heatwaves ease.",
